Number of 1 Bits (Hamming Weight)

Solution 1

class Solution {
    public int hammingWeight(int n) {
        int c=0;
        if(n==0)
            return 0;
        while(n>0)
        {
           if(n%2==1)
               c++;
            n=n/2;
            
        }
        return c;
        
    }
}

Solution 2: Brian Kernighan's Algorithm

java
public class Solution {
    // you need to treat n as an unsigned value
    public int hammingWeight(int n) {
        int ans = 0;
        while (n != 0) {
            n &= n - 1;
            ++ans;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}

Logic:
Subtracting 1 from n flips its lowest 1 bit to 0 and turns all the 0s after it into 1s. When we AND this with n, those bits cancel out, so the lowest 1 bit is removed. Each loop removes one 1 bit and adds 1 to the count. When n becomes 0, the count is the answer.

Example: n = 12 (1100)

1100 & 1011 = 1000 → ans = 1
1000 & 0111 = 0000 → ans = 2

The check is n != 0 (not n > 0), so negative numbers also work.
Time: O(k), where k is the number of 1 bits (at most 32).

Solution 3: Lowest Set Bit Removal

java
public class Solution {
    // you need to treat n as an unsigned value
    public int hammingWeight(int n) {
        int ans = 0;
        while (n != 0) {
            n -= (n & -n);
            ++ans;
        }
        return ans;
    }
}

Logic:
In Java, -n is stored as "flip all bits of n, then add 1". So n & -n keeps only the lowest 1 bit of n. Subtracting this value from n removes that bit. Each loop removes one 1 bit and adds 1 to the count. When n becomes 0, the count is the answer.

Example: n = 12 (1100)

n & -n = 0100, so 1100 − 0100 = 1000 → ans = 1
n & -n = 1000, so 1000 − 1000 = 0000 → ans = 2

Time: O(k), where k is the number of 1 bits (at most 32).
